<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Decelea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dekeleia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Decelea was a deme of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. In 413 BCE, on the advice of the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> occupied and fortified it as a permanent base, cutting Athens off from its farmland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurium</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines and causing thousands of slaves to desert. The last phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> is often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decelean-war">Decelean War</a>.</p></div>
